/**
 * PROSTATE CANCER: THE UK AND NHS LAYER. Facts checked 2026-09-25 against the pages cited on each row.
 *
 * Prostate cancer is the most common cancer in men in the UK and the one where the argument about screening is
 * loudest. What is here: the UK National Screening Committee's March 2026 recommendation, read from the committee's
 * own coversheet rather than from news coverage, which for the first time recommends a screening programme and
 * confines it to men with a pathogenic BRCA2 variant and a family history; the figures on risk in Black men with
 * their cohorts and their original sources; the diagnostic pathway from the age-specific PSA thresholds in NICE
 * NG12, through multiparametric MRI read on a Likert scale and local anaesthetic transperineal biopsy, to the
 * Cambridge Prognostic Group; every NICE decision on a prostate cancer medicine read from nice.org.uk with the
 * Scottish Medicines Consortium's position beside it, including the refusals; the waiting-time figures for
 * urological and prostate cancer by nation and by route, which are the worst of any common cancer in England and
 * Scotland; the National Prostate Cancer Audit's eight indicators and the provider ranges behind them; the surgical
 * and radiotherapy centres with their volumes, read from the audit's own workbook; the UK trial legacy, which for
 * prostate cancer is unusually large (ProtecT, STAMPEDE, CHHiP, PACE-B, PROMIS); the trials open now including
 * TRANSFORM; Prostate Cancer UK and the other charities; and where the four nations differ.
 *
 * No figure is remembered: each carries the page it was read from. Where a page could not be read, the gap is
 * named with the response the server gave.
 *
 * The cancer record is `prostate` (src/data/cancers.ts); the risk-group and disease-state records in
 * src/data/prostate-subtypes.ts are aliases, because screening, the waiting-time standard, the tests and the
 * centres are the same whichever comes back, and the family page is where a reader lands. This file adds Prostate
 * Cancer UK, five UK researchers from their public pages, two trial records the corpus did not have (PROMIS and
 * TRANSFORM) and the `ukPathway`.
 */
